<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    What the share sheet is allowed to mint a content Uri for.

    Exactly the two transfer folders, and deliberately NOT the external-files
    root that contains them. The root also holds Disks/ and ModifiedDisks/ -
    the user's downloaded disk library and its saved modifications - and there
    is no share action anywhere in this app for a disk image. With
    android:exported="false" a root entry would not hand another app anything
    on its own, so this is hygiene rather than a live hole: it means a future
    getUriForFile() call with a wrong File argument fails loudly with
    IllegalArgumentException instead of quietly granting read access to a
    500 KB disk image somebody spent a download on.

    external-files-path, not external-path or files-path: this element is the
    one that resolves against getExternalFilesDir(null), which is where
    transferDir() in HostTransfer.kt puts both folders. files-path points at
    getFilesDir() and would throw for every single share.

    The imports entry is earned, not symmetry: the File Transfer screen offers
    Share on a staged import as well as on an export, so both folders really do
    produce Uris. Nothing here is a path INTO the sandbox - a file arriving
    from another app comes as a Uri minted by the sender's provider and is read
    through contentResolver, and this provider plays no part in that.
-->
<paths>
    <external-files-path name="exports" path="Exports/" />
    <external-files-path name="imports" path="Imports/" />
</paths>
